Umbra-Baile

Creative Catalyst: It descends from carnival musicians who perform only during solar eclipses.

Umbra-Baile is the sound of a drum kit malfunctioning in the presence of a dying star. It strips the high-octane aggression of Baile-Funk, replacing the abrasive snares with the wet, organic thwack of dried palm leaves hitting a wooden floor. The Roland TR-909 is processed through 1970s tape-saturation loops, creating a "collapsed" rhythm that feels like a heart skipping beats during total darkness. It is surreal, claustrophobic, and intensely hallucinogenic.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 125–130 BPM, but with "stutter-gait" programming that drags against the grid, mimicking the physiological unease of eclipse-induced twilight.
*   Harmonic Palette: Diminished-seventh chords and microtonal shifts derived from detuned 1970s analog synths; favors the "Lydian-b3" scale for a queasy, mystical lift.
*   Production Signatures: 
    1.  Corona-Gating: Applying a noise gate that triggers only when the master volume peaks, creating rhythmic holes of silence. 
    2.  Copper-Plate Reverb: Physically running signals through a rusted 1970s copper sheet instead of digital processors.
    3.  Tape-Drag Elasticity: Varying the playback speed of the 909-kick in real-time to mimic gravitational warping.
*   Dynamics: A slow, ritualistic crawl that suddenly explodes into "blinding" harmonic dissonance before abruptly collapsing into complete silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the mountainous villages of the high-altitude tropics, the "Eclipse Guild" wait years for the moon to swallow the sun. Their fashion is a deliberate defiance of sight: mirrored, reflective jumpsuits that blind onlookers, paired with ceremonial headpieces made of salvaged 1970s radio antennae. When the sun vanishes, they occupy the sudden cold with a frantic, rhythmic devotion. They do not record music; they trap the "dead air" of the eclipse onto aging tape reels, believing that if the rhythm stops before the sun returns, the light will never come back.

Typical Instruments: Roland TR-909 (heavily tape-distorted), 1970s ARP 2600 (used for sweeping, pitch-bent leads), processed field recordings of jungle birds, and hammered scrap-metal percussion.

For Fans Of: Arca, Shuggie Otis (deconstructed), Brazilian Bass experiments, Sun Ra (late-period).

Fictional Band Name: The Parallax Orchestra.

Band Description: A four-piece ensemble consisting of a percussionist who plays exclusively on obsidian stones and a lead programmer hidden behind a mountain of patch cables. They dress in hazmat-style white silk suits and are famous for performing only in pitch-black arenas where the audience is prohibited from using light sources of any kind. Their sound is claustrophobic, tactile, and sounds like the internal architecture of a machine that has developed a nervous system.

